  </wide-block>

  \;

  Relationship between FS & FT analysis:

  <\equation*>
    X<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>=<below|<above|<big|sum>|\<infty\>>|k=-\<infty\>>2\<pi\>a<rsub|k> \<delta\><around*|(|w-k\<omega\><rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  If there is an aperiodic signal <math|g<around*|(|t|)>>, how to compute its FS?

  Replicate <math|g<around*|(|t|)>> in a period of <math|T<rsub|0>> (<math|T<rsub|0>> is not the length of <math|g<around*|(|t|)>>), then

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|k>=<frac|1|T<rsub|0>><big|int><rsub|T<rsub|0>>g<around*|(|t|)>e<rsup|-j k w<rsub|0> t>\<mathd\>t.
  </equation*>

  Consider its FT:

  <\equation*>
    G<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>=<big|int><rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|-\<infty\>>g<around*|(|t|)>e<rsup|-j\<omega\> t>\<mathd\>t.
  </equation*>

  So

  <\equation*>
    <frame|a<rsub|k>=<frac|G<around*|(|j k\<omega\><rsub|0>|)>|T<rsub|0>>>.
  </equation*>

  which means <math|a<rsub|k>> is sampling on <math|G<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>> with <math|k> then divided by <math|T<rsub|0>>.

  When the sampling is denser, the <math|a<rsub|k>> approximates <math|G<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>> more, then <math|T<rsub|0>\<rightarrow\>\<infty\>> more.

  \;

  If <math|x<around*|(|t|)>> is real (i.e. <math|x<around*|(|t|)>=x<rprime|\<asterisk\>><around*|(|t|)>>), then

  <\equation*>
    X<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>=X<rprime|\<asterisk\>><around*|(|-j\<omega\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    <frame|\<cal-E\>v<around*|{|x<around*|(|t|)>|}><above|\<longleftrightarrow\>|\<cal*-F\>>\<cal-R\>e<around*|{|X<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>|}>>.
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    <frame|\<cal-O\>d<around*|{|x<around*|(|t|)>|}><above|\<longleftrightarrow\>|\<cal*-F\>>j\<cal-I\>m<around*|{|X<around*|(|j\<omega\>|)>|}>>.
  </equation*>

  \;
